WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.352
Welcome to Module 1 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.352 --> 00:00:07.250
name one writer for every account state.

3
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.888
The HR pipeline makes an account exist.

4
00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:13.540
This path teaches what happens
to that account afterward,

5
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.725
how the HR record decides every
change, and how you prove that it did.

6
00:00:17.725 --> 00:00:23.523
There are seven modules, one for each gate
of the HR-driven identity implementation

7
00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:28.900
guide, and this one is Gate 1, Writers,
with Chapter 01 of the developer workbook.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.975
It comes before every other gate.

9
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:34.150
In it you'll read the lifecycle
rows of a writer register,

10
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.325
check the register's shape,
and scan a month of directory

11
00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:40.500
audit records for writes by
anyone but the named writer.

12
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.913
The code runs on your laptop
against fictional fixtures.

13
00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:49.600
It's illustrative: unit-tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.825
Why does this come first?

15
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:56.475
Picture three writers.

16
00:00:56.475 --> 00:00:59.375
Your HR feed sets an
account's active value.

17
00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:02.775
A Lifecycle Workflows leaver
can disable the same account.

18
00:01:02.775 --> 00:01:06.925
And a help desk technician can still
edit a user's organization code.

19
00:01:06.925 --> 00:01:08.502
Each of those is a writer,

20
00:01:08.502 --> 00:01:12.287
and when two of them write the
same value, the later one wins.

21
00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:16.838
So a late active true from the pipeline
undoes the workflow's disable.

22
00:01:16.838 --> 00:01:19.698
A help desk edit to the organization code

23
00:01:19.698 --> 00:01:22.726
moves the person's access,
and HR never knows.

24
00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:25.825
In this module you fix that
before any other gate.

25
00:01:25.825 --> 00:01:30.826
You name one writer, and a backstop, for
every account-state transition and every

26
00:01:30.826 --> 00:01:36.030
HR-mastered value, and then you test the
directory audit log against that list.

27
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.591
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:42.591 --> 00:01:44.955
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:44.955 --> 00:01:49.330
You'll explain why one writer per account
state comes before every other gate.

30
00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.519
You'll read the lifecycle
rows of the writer register

31
00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:55.780
and say which writer and
which backstop each row names.

32
00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.055
You'll run the register check and the
audit-log scan, and read what they find.

33
00:02:00.055 --> 00:02:03.180
And you'll say what the scan
can't see, and what covers it.

34
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.484
Before you start, you need
the path's prerequisites:

35
00:02:06.484 --> 00:02:11.753
at least the overview of the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 03, Microsoft

36
00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:16.630
Automation Modules 1 and 2, and enough
Python to run a script and read JSON.

37
00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.239
Lab 1 needs Python 3.10 or later,

38
00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:23.518
pytest and a clone of the
repository, and no tenant.

39
00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:28.700
Lab 2 needs a worldwide or GCC
test tenant, never production.

40
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.500
Here's the register
you'll check in the lab.

41
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.838
It's the guide's table of lifecycle
rows, kept as reviewed JSON.

42
00:02:38.838 --> 00:02:43.273
Each row is an account-state
transition or an HR-mastered value,

43
00:02:43.273 --> 00:02:47.963
and each names exactly one writer
and, where there is one, a backstop.

44
00:02:47.963 --> 00:02:50.387
Inbound provisioning creates the account.

45
00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:54.668
It enables it at hire, disables it at
separation, and re-enables it if a

46
00:02:54.668 --> 00:02:59.788
separation is cancelled, with Lifecycle
Workflows as the backstop for all three.

47
00:02:59.788 --> 00:03:03.200
It writes the hire date, the
leave date and the manager.

48
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.537
The organization code and worker
type code have the directory sync as

49
00:03:07.537 --> 00:03:11.875
an interim backstop, for synced
users until Active Directory retires.

50
00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:16.000
The card management system's integration
writes the certificate binding.

51
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.611
And notice deletion: the
post-separation workflow writes it,

52
00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:21.850
and inbound provisioning never does.

53
00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.720
The register forbids it.

54
00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:32.375
The register names four writers, and
each one is a named group of identities,

55
00:03:32.375 --> 00:03:34.670
so the scan can tell who made a write.

56
00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.332
Inbound provisioning is the HR
app and the contractor-source app,

57
00:03:38.332 --> 00:03:40.570
which come through the same middleware.

58
00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:45.945
Lifecycle Workflows is the set of workflow
tasks that enable, disable and delete.

59
00:03:45.945 --> 00:03:51.107
The card management integration writes the
PIV binding, which is Gate 5's business.

60
00:03:51.107 --> 00:03:53.656
And the sync agent is an interim writer,

61
00:03:53.656 --> 00:03:56.920
for synced users until
Active Directory retires.

62
00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.058
Why does Book 03 treat this so seriously?

63
00:04:00.058 --> 00:04:04.033
Because whoever can write a slot
decides what a rule on that slot grants.

64
00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:06.157
A writer is an access decision.

65
00:04:06.157 --> 00:04:09.696
And the pipeline's active value
and the workflows' tasks can

66
00:04:09.696 --> 00:04:13.307
both write account state, so
without a register, they collide.

67
00:04:13.307 --> 00:04:17.380
The register is signed, and
carries a versioned scope filter.

68
00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.580
The kit checks the register in two ways.

69
00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.507
The first is the register check,
rules W R oh one to oh nine,

70
00:04:27.507 --> 00:04:31.093
which test the register's
shape before you trust it.

71
00:04:31.093 --> 00:04:34.492
A row must name a single
writer and appear only once.

72
00:04:34.492 --> 00:04:37.367
Every writer and backstop must be defined.

73
00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.762
A writer with no
identities gets a warning,

74
00:04:39.762 --> 00:04:42.430
because the scan couldn't
attribute its writes.

75
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.142
Every required account-state
transition, created,

76
00:04:46.142 --> 00:04:49.943
enabled, disabled, re-enabled
and deleted, needs a row.

77
00:04:49.943 --> 00:04:53.280
Deletion must never be written
by inbound provisioning.

78
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.655
A backstop can't be the writer itself.

79
00:04:55.655 --> 00:04:59.605
Two rows for the same audited change
can't name different writers.

80
00:04:59.605 --> 00:05:03.955
And the register should be recorded as
signed, with a versioned scope filter.

81
00:05:03.955 --> 00:05:07.474
Every tool in the kit
uses the same exit codes:

82
00:05:07.474 --> 00:05:12.600
zero when clean, two for findings
or warnings, one for an error.

83
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.225
The second check is the scan.

84
00:05:18.225 --> 00:05:20.980
It reads an export of
the directory audit log,

85
00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.629
keeps the successful records
inside the window you give it,

86
00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:29.246
and matches each one to a row of the
register: by the activity, such as Disable

87
00:05:29.246 --> 00:05:34.012
account, and for an Update user record,
by the name of the property that changed.

88
00:05:34.012 --> 00:05:35.849
Then it judges who made the write.

89
00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:37.425
The row's writer is fine.

90
00:05:37.425 --> 00:05:40.159
The backstop gets W S oh two, a warning:

91
00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:43.987
confirm the writer acted, or
find out why it was late.

92
00:05:43.987 --> 00:05:48.888
Anyone else is W S oh one, a second
writer, and that's a finding.

93
00:05:48.888 --> 00:05:50.891
And a writer the row forbids,

94
00:05:50.891 --> 00:05:55.249
like inbound provisioning deleting
an account, is W S oh four.

95
00:05:55.249 --> 00:06:00.464
The scan writes an output folder with
a manifest that holds each file's hash,

96
00:06:00.464 --> 00:06:04.260
and a saved query for every
row, which Module 7 runs.

97
00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:08.973
Two limits.

98
00:06:08.973 --> 00:06:11.022
First, what the scan can't see.

99
00:06:11.022 --> 00:06:14.297
For synced users, a write
made in Active Directory

100
00:06:14.297 --> 00:06:17.572
never reaches the Entra
audit log this scan reads.

101
00:06:17.572 --> 00:06:20.773
Only Active Directory's own
change auditing sees it.

102
00:06:20.773 --> 00:06:25.355
The guide's interim path covers that:
it names the sync agent as a writer in

103
00:06:25.355 --> 00:06:30.397
the register, and reviews the access
control lists on the organizational units.

104
00:06:30.397 --> 00:06:32.723
Second, what the scan can't confirm.

105
00:06:32.723 --> 00:06:37.522
To match an Update user record, the
register names the property that changed.

106
00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:41.884
Learn doesn't document those names,
so each one is the program's reading.

107
00:06:41.884 --> 00:06:45.473
If a name is wrong, the scan
reports nothing for that value,

108
00:06:45.473 --> 00:06:48.297
and silence looks exactly
like a clean month.

109
00:06:48.297 --> 00:06:51.672
That's why Lab 2 matters more
here than in most modules.

110
00:06:51.672 --> 00:06:54.390
Confirm every property name against a real

111
00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:57.770
audit record before the
scan counts as evidence.

112
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.245
Try it.

113
00:07:02.245 --> 00:07:07.405
Lab 1 runs in the hr-lifecycle folder
of the examples, on fictional fixtures,

114
00:07:07.405 --> 00:07:11.233
and nothing in it signs in to
anything or sends a request.

115
00:07:11.233 --> 00:07:14.807
Step one lists what the scan
reads, and with what permission.

116
00:07:14.807 --> 00:07:18.402
The list option prints the task's
calls without making them:

117
00:07:18.402 --> 00:07:23.243
two GET calls against the audit logs,
the directory audits and the provisioning

118
00:07:23.243 --> 00:07:27.570
logs, which need AuditLog dot Read dot
All and Directory dot Read dot All.

119
00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:30.745
Those are the only two
permissions a real run would need.

120
00:07:30.745 --> 00:07:33.195
Step two checks the program's register.

121
00:07:33.195 --> 00:07:38.468
It comes back with zero findings, zero
warnings and zero information items,

122
00:07:38.468 --> 00:07:43.145
and an exit code of zero, so the
register's shape passes every W R rule.

123
00:07:43.145 --> 00:07:45.275
That's the precondition for the scan:

124
00:07:45.275 --> 00:07:49.460
a register you've checked is one
you can test the audit log against.

125
00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:57.060
Step three scans October's audit
records against the register.

126
00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.977
You give it the register, the audit
export, a window from the first

127
00:08:00.977 --> 00:08:04.672
to the thirty-first of October,
the cloud, and an output folder.

128
00:08:04.672 --> 00:08:11.860
The cloud option has no default; G C C,
G C C High or D O D picks the Graph host.

129
00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.298
The scan found eleven protected writes.

130
00:08:14.298 --> 00:08:17.685
Five were by the writer the row
names, which is what you want.

131
00:08:17.685 --> 00:08:19.160
One was by the backstop.

132
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.960
Four were by a second writer, someone
the register doesn't name at all.

133
00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.735
And one was by a writer
the register forbids.

134
00:08:25.735 --> 00:08:29.760
That's five findings and one
warning, so the exit code is two.

135
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.127
The output folder's manifest
lists every file's hash,

136
00:08:33.127 --> 00:08:35.085
so the run can be checked later.

137
00:08:35.085 --> 00:08:38.120
The output on the slide is wrapped to fit.

138
00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.095
Step four prints the
findings, one line each.

139
00:08:45.095 --> 00:08:50.105
Four are W S oh one, second writers: a
help desk technician disabled an account,

140
00:08:50.105 --> 00:08:53.206
an Exchange administrator
edited a leave date,

141
00:08:53.206 --> 00:08:58.295
the help desk changed a manager, and a
fix-up script wrote an organization code.

142
00:08:58.295 --> 00:09:03.245
None of them is inbound provisioning,
the writer every one of those rows names.

143
00:09:03.245 --> 00:09:07.639
One is W S oh four: inbound
provisioning deleted an account,

144
00:09:07.639 --> 00:09:09.745
which the register forbids.

145
00:09:09.745 --> 00:09:14.312
And one is the W S oh two warning:
the backstop, Lifecycle Workflows,

146
00:09:14.312 --> 00:09:19.470
enabled a hire, so you confirm the pipeline
acted or find out why it was late.

147
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:24.563
Step five prints one saved query, the
third, for the disabled-at-separation row:

148
00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:27.587
a filter on Disable
account inside the window,

149
00:09:27.587 --> 00:09:30.532
with the pipeline and
the workflows allowed.

150
00:09:30.532 --> 00:09:36.140
Step six runs the tests, fifty-seven
pass, and you delete the output folder.

151
00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:43.988
Lab 2 runs the same scan
against your own test tenant,

152
00:09:43.988 --> 00:09:46.752
worldwide or G C C, never production.

153
00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:49.164
Make two known writes in the admin center,

154
00:09:49.164 --> 00:09:51.789
then export the audit
window that holds them.

155
00:09:51.789 --> 00:09:54.448
Before you scan, confirm
the property names

156
00:09:54.448 --> 00:09:57.327
the register expects
against the real records.

157
00:09:57.327 --> 00:10:00.303
That's the step that turns
the scan into evidence.

158
00:10:00.303 --> 00:10:05.440
Then put your tenant's service principal
I D's in a copy of the register, and scan.

159
00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:11.052
Both of your admin center changes should
come back as W S oh one, second writers.

160
00:10:11.052 --> 00:10:15.698
The reader app needs a certificate and
two application permissions with admin

161
00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:20.415
consent, AuditLog dot Read dot All and
Directory dot Read dot All, and nothing else.

162
00:10:20.415 --> 00:10:24.925
To back it out, restore the test
users' values, remove the permissions,

163
00:10:24.925 --> 00:10:28.952
and delete the app, its certificate,
the export and the output.

164
00:10:28.952 --> 00:10:31.450
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

165
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:36.587
The federal requirement.

166
00:10:36.587 --> 00:10:41.503
This gate meets NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

167
00:10:41.503 --> 00:10:46.576
account management, and I A four, identifier
management, through the register.

168
00:10:46.576 --> 00:10:48.644
And it meets A U six, audit review,

169
00:10:48.644 --> 00:10:53.737
by reviewing writes made outside the
named writer, which is what the scan does.

170
00:10:53.737 --> 00:10:58.637
The guide's Gate 1 requirements table and
the workbook chapter carry the details.

171
00:10:58.637 --> 00:11:02.262
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 1: the signed register,

172
00:11:02.262 --> 00:11:05.808
with its lifecycle rows and
the versioned scope filter;

173
00:11:05.808 --> 00:11:09.431
the output of the register
check; the scan's output folder,

174
00:11:09.431 --> 00:11:14.828
with its manifest and the saved queries;
and the Active Directory auditing settings,

175
00:11:14.828 --> 00:11:16.601
which this code doesn't read,

176
00:11:16.601 --> 00:11:20.590
because writes in Active
Directory never reach the scan.

177
00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.315
Check yourself with two questions.

178
00:11:26.315 --> 00:11:31.190
First: your HR feed and the leaver
workflow can both disable an account.

179
00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:34.578
What does the register record for
the disabled-at-separation row?

180
00:11:34.578 --> 00:11:39.352
The answer is b: one writer, with
the other recorded as its backstop.

181
00:11:39.352 --> 00:11:42.622
The register check enforces
it: one writer per row,

182
00:11:42.622 --> 00:11:45.414
and a backstop that
isn't the writer itself.

183
00:11:45.414 --> 00:11:49.115
Second: which writes does the
Entra audit-log scan not see?

184
00:11:49.115 --> 00:11:53.465
The answer is c: writes made in
Active Directory for synced users.

185
00:11:53.465 --> 00:11:58.291
Only Active Directory's change auditing
sees those, and the guide's interim

186
00:11:58.291 --> 00:12:03.040
path names the sync agent as a writer
and reviews the access control lists.

187
00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.290
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

188
00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:12.865
To sum up.

189
00:12:12.865 --> 00:12:17.932
You checked a writer register's shape,
scanned a month of audit records against it,

190
00:12:17.932 --> 00:12:20.427
and read the saved query that Gate 7 runs.

191
00:12:20.427 --> 00:12:23.004
Along the way you saw four second writers,

192
00:12:23.004 --> 00:12:26.490
one forbidden delete, and one
backstop that acted first.

193
00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:30.414
Carry one thing forward: inbound
provisioning writes the leave

194
00:12:30.414 --> 00:12:34.115
date and the active value, and
that's true for contractors too.

195
00:12:34.115 --> 00:12:38.165
Next is Module 2: run contractors
from their own source.

196
00:12:38.165 --> 00:12:43.702
Contractors don't come from HR, and an end
date nobody updates keeps access alive.

197
00:12:43.702 --> 00:12:48.737
In Module 2 you'll compute each contractor's
earliest end, reconcile the register,

198
00:12:48.737 --> 00:12:52.569
the card system and the directory
without changing anything,

199
00:12:52.569 --> 00:12:56.920
and write a sponsor review that
removes access when nobody responds.
